# wrapped_shared_no_early_free.tkr — the COL-F0c wrapped-refcount fixture (crumb 0023). `main.tks`
# retains one object twice (a collection insert plus an external binding) then releases it once (a
# collection `remove`): the count drops from 2 to 1, so the object must NOT be freed, proven by a
# fresh `region_new` NOT recycling the object's header. It then `place`s and `read`s a value THROUGH
# the surviving reference, proving the external binding stays valid and writable, not just
# unfreed. A final `release` drops the count to zero, proven this time by a fresh `region_new`
# recycling the object's exact header address. Exits 0 only if the object survives the shared
# release and is freed exactly when its last reference drops. The compiler holds no `wrapped`
# objects, so this path is not fixpoint-exercised. Pinned to the C backend.

Feature: wrapped_shared_no_early_free — release from one holder never frees an object other holders share

  Scenario: remove_decrements_but_the_external_binding_keeps_the_object_alive_and_writable
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
